  "summary": "Recent SARS-CoV-2 and influenza studies pair viral sequence features with computational classification or antigenic prediction methods.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational analysis of viral sequence variation linked to a classification or phenotype readout. A preprint encodes direct coupling analysis of epistatic mutations as Circos images and applies convolutional neural networks to classify SARS-CoV-2 variants (Alpha, Delta, Omicron, Else). FluNexus, a preprint platform, preprocesses HA1 and HI data for H1, H3, and H5 influenza A viruses and performs online antigenic prediction and cartography using a manifold-based approach. A published study reports that IFITM3 deficiency drives SARS-CoV-2 adaptation across animal species while preserving variant-specific traits. A database of PCR primers targets human respiratory viruses, and a preprint applies a statistical framework to detect TCR clonal expansion after SARS-CoV-2 vaccination. What is new is the encoding of epistatic coupling as image input for variant classification. The summaries do not establish which residue-level or conformational features drive the observed variation, so mechanistic claims remain open.",
